From: EglantinePS5 <noreply@eglantineps5.local>
Subject: [PATCH] oot: Max Resolution on by default, and the copy buffers that paid for it

Ocarina of Time's built-in Max Resolution patch takes the game's "dynamic resolution disabled" path
so the 3D renders at full scale instead of following the GPU-time controller. Lichii has it on; this
port had it opt-in for one stated reason - "the bigger render targets come out of the same memory
pool that ends a session".

That reason was measured against this build and does not hold up as the cause. The newest 145 s PS5
run has the patch OFF and still finishes with VMA heap 1 at 6274 MiB of 6422, 148 MiB free, deleting
3615 images and downloading 669 in its last 600-frame window, with the present frame time gone from
32 ms to 61 ms. The tightness is there without the patch. What causes it is the fixed pair
EDEN_NX2_TC_EXPECTED_MIB / EDEN_NX2_TC_CRITICAL_MIB: they overwrite the collector's thresholds after
it has computed them from the live budget, margin and block slack, which is the one calculation that
knows how much room the session actually has. Unset, that calculation runs, and the pressure margin
comes off its result.

Against that, this patch's own cost is small and now known: one 32-bit render target at 1920x1080 is
7.9 MiB against 1.6 MiB at the 854x480 floor, so about +6.3 MiB, and a colour/depth pair about
+12.7 MiB - roughly one texture, against the ~1 GiB the adaptive thresholds keep back.

So the table is on unless EDEN_NX2_OOT_MAX_RESOLUTION=0, and the copy-buffer retention goes from 60
frames to 600. A copy buffer is one per power of two, sized to the largest copy of that class, so at
60 frames a 2560x1440 one (16 MiB) and a 4096x4096 one (64 MiB) were destroyed and made again
repeatedly. 600 is the value the upstream build was tested at, and it bounds them, where unset used
to mean "kept until the process ends".

What this can break. If the heap is still tight after the overrides are unset, this adds pressure on
top of it rather than being neutral, and the failure looks like the older log - "Out of device
memory: keeping N MiB more free" repeating and the thresholds walking down. The escape is
EDEN_NX2_OOT_MAX_RESOLUTION=0, one line in env.txt, no new build.

What it deliberately does not do. The analysis proposes a further step: protecting the active
full-scale colour/depth images from the collector and allocating them dedicated. It is not here
because its evidence is inference - the churn counters count all texture-cache images, and the report
could not show the full-scale targets are the churned ones - and because exempting images the wrong
way "would convert a cache pressure problem into an immediate heap failure". If the console session
shows pressure, that is the next change, not this one.

Measured, not a guess: the heap figures, the churn counts and the frame times are from that log; the
per-target MiB are computed from the formats' 32 bits per pixel at 854x480 and 1920x1080. The effect
of turning the patch on is not yet console-tested. The proof lines are "Applying built-in patch "Max
Resolution (built-in)" at main+0x805bd0" from patch_manager.cpp, the "Memory margins: base ...,
out of memory ..., block slack ..." line, and the absence of "Out of device memory: keeping N MiB
more free".

diff --git a/src/core/file_sys/patch_manager.cpp b/src/core/file_sys/patch_manager.cpp
index 1245039624..caa2f06a1d 100644
--- a/src/core/file_sys/patch_manager.cpp
+++ b/src/core/file_sys/patch_manager.cpp
@@ -73,12 +73,17 @@ constexpr std::array BUILTIN_PATCHES{
     },
 };
 
-// EDEN_NX2_OOT_MAX_RESOLUTION=1 applies the table. Lichii has it on by default; here it is opt-in,
-// because the bigger render targets come out of the same memory pool that ends a session.
+// On by default, as Lichii has it. The bigger render targets do come out of the same memory pool that
+// ends a session, so this went in opt-in; what changed is that the pool's tightness was measured to
+// be there with the patch off - VMA heap 6274 MiB of 6422 in a 145 s run, 148 MiB free - and to be
+// the fixed EDEN_NX2_TC_EXPECTED_MIB / EDEN_NX2_TC_CRITICAL_MIB overrides defeating the collector's
+// own budget calculation, not this patch. One 32-bit render target costs about 6.3 MiB more than the
+// 854x480 floor and a colour/depth pair about 12.7 MiB, against the ~1 GiB the adaptive thresholds
+// keep back. EDEN_NX2_OOT_MAX_RESOLUTION=0 turns the table off.
 [[nodiscard]] bool BuiltinPatchesEnabled() {
     static const bool enabled = [] {
         const char* const value = std::getenv("EDEN_NX2_OOT_MAX_RESOLUTION");
-        return value != nullptr && value[0] == '1';
+        return value == nullptr || value[0] != '0';
     }();
     return enabled;
 }
diff --git a/src/video_core/renderer_vulkan/vk_texture_cache.cpp b/src/video_core/renderer_vulkan/vk_texture_cache.cpp
index 55ec1a18b0..454f950d93 100644
--- a/src/video_core/renderer_vulkan/vk_texture_cache.cpp
+++ b/src/video_core/renderer_vulkan/vk_texture_cache.cpp
@@ -2797,13 +2797,18 @@ void TextureCacheRuntime::TickFrame() {
     // buffer from GetTemporaryBuffer (one per power of two, the size of the largest copy of that
     // class so far) that no copy has used for this many frames, and that the GPU is past, is
     // destroyed; the next copy of that size makes it again.
+    // 600 by default: at 60 a copy buffer was destroyed and made again repeatedly, and 600 is the
+    // value the upstream build was tested at. Unset no longer means "kept for the session", which is
+    // what made a 2560x1440 copy 16 MiB and a 4096x4096 one 64 MiB until the process ended; =0 still
+    // asks for that.
     static const u32 temp_buffer_frames = [] {
         const char* const value = std::getenv("EDEN_NX2_TC_TEMP_BUFFER_FRAMES");
-        const u32 frames = value != nullptr ? static_cast<u32>(std::strtoul(value, nullptr, 10)) : 0U;
-        if (frames != 0) {
-            LOG_INFO(Render_Vulkan, "EDEN_NX2_TC_TEMP_BUFFER_FRAMES={}: copy buffers unused for {} frames are destroyed",
-                     value, frames);
-        }
+        const u32 frames =
+            value != nullptr ? static_cast<u32>(std::strtoul(value, nullptr, 10)) : 600U;
+        LOG_INFO(Render_Vulkan,
+                 "EDEN_NX2_TC_TEMP_BUFFER_FRAMES={}: copy buffers unused for {} frames are "
+                 "destroyed",
+                 value != nullptr ? value : "600 (default)", frames);
         return frames;
     }();
     if (temp_buffer_frames != 0) {
